<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">1931 Constitution of Ethiopia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as first written constitution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1931 Constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> was the first written constitution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, promulgated by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> in 1931. It created a bicameral parliament with limited powers while confirming the emperor's supreme authority and the dynasty's claim to rule. It is generally seen as an instrument of centralisation and modernisation from above rather than a genuine limitation of imperial power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/1931-constitution-of-ethiopia.jpg" alt="1931 Constitution of Ethiopia" width="297" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tekle Hawariat Tekle Mariyam Gedamu Woldegiorgis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1931_Constitution_of_Ethiopia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The constitution was meant to replace the Fetha Nagast, the supreme law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Haile Selassie wrote that as regent he had wanted Empress Zewditu to proclaim one, but some great nobles who profited from ruling without a constitution claimed it would diminish her dignity. As emperor he appointed a drafting commission whose leading members included the Europeans Gaston Jèze and Johannes Kolmodin and, most prominently, the Ethiopian intellectuals Tekle Hawariat Tekle Mariyam and Gedamu Woldegiorgis. It was modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and promulgated on 16 July 1931, its adoption pressed in part by international opinion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The text is a decree followed by 55 articles in seven chapters. The first reserved the throne to descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>, whose line was traced to Menelik I, son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a>, and the emperor's person was declared sacred and his power indisputable. Subjects' rights held only &quot;except in the cases provided by law&quot; and did not limit the emperor in war or public misfortune.</p><p>The chapter on the chambers created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s first formal legislature. The emperor appointed the upper chamber, while the lower was to be chosen by the nobility and the local chiefs until the people could elect it. Further chapters covered the ministers, the courts, including the Special Courts required by the Klobukowski agreement of 1906, and an annual budget.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>To explain the novelty, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> called officials and notables to an assembly at which Tekle Hawariat lectured on constitutional law. The first parliament met on 3 November, the anniversary of the emperor's coronation; according to the historian Harold Marcus he hoped it would stimulate nationalism and unity. After his restoration in 1941 Haile Selassie revived the constitution and convened parliament on 2 November 1942, with a chamber of deputies twice its pre-war size, though he still issued many laws by proclamation and decree. A new constitution superseded it at his Silver Jubilee in 1955.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “1931 Constitution of Ethiopia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Haile Selassie introduced Ethiopia's <b>first written constitution</b> in 1931 and formally abolished slavery in 1942, but the feudal land system and his personal concentration of authority prevented the institutional modernisation needed to address rural poverty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Abu Bakar (r. 1862–1895), recognised by Britain as Sultan of Johor in 1885, created a modern administration with government departments, a state council and the Johor constitution of 1895, one of the <b>first written constitutions</b> in the Malay world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The document is often called the <b>first written constitution</b> in the Western tradition.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-14">14 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In December 1653, after the failure of Barebone's Parliament, army officers drew up the Instrument of Government, Britain's <b>first written constitution</b>, which made Oliver Cromwell Lord Protector of the Commonwealth of England, Scotland and Ireland, with a Council of State and Parliament.</blockquote><a href="/state/the-protectorate">The Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
